.TH MPM 1 "2026-11-07" "Mojt Linux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpm \- manage Mojt content, Ports, catalogs and native boot choices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpm
.RI [ global-options ] " ports " action " " [ arguments ]
.br
.B mpm
.RI [ --root " DIRECTORY" ] " kernel " install|status|plan " " [ arguments ]
.br
.B mpm
.RI "kernel " stage|select|rollback|status " [--disk-root DIRECTORY] " [ arguments ]
.br
.B mpm
.RI "system " install|status " [--disk-root DIRECTORY] " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
MPM is Mojt's experimental package manager. The Nix-backed
.B ports
area manages shared command-line applications retained side by side in the Nix store.
Imports use binary substitutes only; unavailable substitutes fail instead
of starting source builds. System services and desktop integration are not
automatically configured for installed applications.
.PP
The shared repository/System frontend also installs exact local native Kernel packages,
inspects their retained registry and checks explicit read-only deployment plans.
These operations require no optional provider backend and never activate a Kernel.
.PP
New System profiles deliver the complete frontend and its Python modules.
Installed commands use the selected System's pinned Python, an isolated import
path and management command path, independently of Ports programs.
Ports defaults to /var/lib/mojt/ports. The installed frontend discovers requested
packages from the System's pinned Nixpkgs source. Explicit finite
mojt-ports-sources-v1 bundles remain available and take precedence.
Accepted metadata is retained in the published profile.
Native Kernel install/plan defaults to /run/mojt-disk/kernel; boot commands
default to /run/mojt-disk. Explicit --root/--disk-root inspection keeps its
explicit target and does not attach the current machine's runtime record.
Installed Kernel status includes selected/running identities when a native
deployment record is available; legacy Systems retain unknown deployment status.
The source-checkout frontend keeps its existing explicit-target behavior.
.PP
Native verification needs the selected System's retained zstd helper; staging
also needs modprobe. Missing tools are prerequisite errors, never implicit
package acquisition or a fallback to a Ports executable. A fresh native
baseline is bound by its runtime record and complete generated menu, not uname.
.PP
Catalog selection, recorded requests, installed artifacts and active command
selections are separate. An artifact identity from
.B list --exact
identifies an installed build; it is not itself an upgrade request.
.SH BASE CONTENT AND NEXT BOOT
.BI "base install --from-file " "ARCHIVE --expected-id sha256:FULL_ID"
retains the verified complete Base and publishes a validated rootfs realization.
Use --dry-run for a read-only target preview or -y to accept that operation.
Alternatively use --source NAME with --expected-id and --archive-sha256 HEX;
--snapshot, --sources-root and --cache-root have the Kernel source meanings.
Source preview reads metadata only and does not claim rootfs verification.
.PP
New System payloads retain GNU tar alongside zstd for the reviewed Base extractor.
Realization preserves numeric owners, modes, supported links and extended metadata;
it requires an administrator. No defaults are copied into machine /etc, no state
is initialized and no current link, boot menu or running content is changed.
The first subset rejects unsupported required dependencies/requirements.
.PP
.BI "base stage --artifact " "sha256:FULL_ID --base-context FILE"
verifies installed Base against selected System/Kernel and actual state, then
retains a private read-only boot tree and bound initramfs. Requires matching
retained System management code and already present configuration defaults.
Missing defaults are reported without changing configuration. --dry-run writes
no slot. Staging never changes next boot or the running deployment. Explicit
Base selection is separate. Confirm the running deployment after reboot.
.PP
.BI "base select --deployment " "sha256:FULL_DEPLOYMENT_ID --expected-selected sha256:CURRENT_ID"
selects a fully staged exact deployment for next boot while retaining System and
Kernel. --dry-run verifies without publication; -y accepts the choice. Both the
candidate and recovery deployment are checked again at publication. A repeated
choice is a verified no-op. Running content is unchanged until reboot.
.PP
.BI "base rollback --expected-selected " "sha256:CURRENT_ID"
selects the previous deployment only if exact System and Kernel are retained.
Both deployments remain available. Rollback does not undo persistent state.
.PP
.B base status
reports installed, next-boot and running Base identities separately. --verify
rechecks retained archive, wrapper and rootfs content/metadata; --json returns
mpm-base-status-v1 with complete IDs and explicit unknown observations.
A cache/archive-only object is retained, not installed Base. Incomplete work and
missing retention behind running/selected observations are reported separately.
.PP
All Base actions accept --disk-root DIRECTORY for one existing installation target.
The source frontend requires it; the installed frontend defaults to its mounted
disk. Explicit --disk-root is offline inspection and never imports the inspection
host's running identity. Global --root is rejected for Base commands. Status
creates no target, registry, lock or selection and needs no provider/refresh.
.PP
Exact import preserves the first receipt on an unchanged repeat, including while
the original archive is unavailable. It does not create a moving upgrade wish.
Base moving upgrades and System deployment remain unimplemented. See
docs/base-installation.md and docs/mpm-base-contract.md for the implemented scope.
MBT public spellings remain proposed; bootstrap will reuse the same MPM functions.
.SH SYSTEM CONTENT
.BI "system install --from-file " "GENERATION --expected-id sha256:FULL_ID"
verifies a native System generation, its exact native component inputs and its
resolved composition, then materializes an immutable profile. Supply missing
components with repeatable --dependency-file ARCHIVE. Installed exact components
are reused; unrelated inputs fail. --dry-run validates without target writes;
-y approves installation. No package code, services or lifecycle actions execute.
.PP
The first subset requires native leaf components with ordinary protected files,
explicit Base ABI facts, the bin/python3, bin/mpm and tools runtime layout, and
an empty lifecycle action set. Native components may bind exact provider runtime
dependencies; the installed frontend imports/verifies Nix binary closures and retains
them without activating a profile. Provider operations require the running installation;
explicit offline targets cannot use the host store. Direct provider profile mappings,
transitive native components and links remain unsupported. No source refresh occurs.
.PP
.BI "system install --source " "NAME --expected-id sha256:ID --archive-sha256 SHA256"
uses one accepted source snapshot and the common cache for the generation and
its exact native components. Source --dry-run is metadata-only and creates no
cache/profile; --snapshot pins accepted metadata. Controlled profile links are
retained. Late-created privileged helpers remain unavailable runtime requirements;
installation does not create them or establish boot readiness. See docs/system-packaging.md.
.PP
.BI "{base,system,kernel} activate --artifact " "sha256:FULL_ID"
prepares an installed exact candidate and atomically selects its complete
native-v2 deployment for next boot. upgrade --artifact ID uses the same path
with an explicitly chosen update target. --dry-run previews without writing
runtime bundles, slots, boot trees or selection. There is no system plan command.
Base requires --base-context FILE declaring Kernel bounds for this exact Base.
The other two areas, running system and persistent state are preserved.
No source refresh, moving-policy resolution, automatic reboot or migration occurs.
.PP
stage --artifact ID prepares without selecting. select --deployment ID
--expected-selected ID chooses an admitted deployment. rollback
--expected-selected ID returns to the previous compatible selection within the
specified area. These lower-level operations remain available.
.PP
The first System update policy preserves startup/services and their existing
provider dependencies, including service libc closures. Tooling changes and
added admin commands are supported; service changes, missing defaults and
unsupported migrations block. Base ABI, Kernel bounds, account shells and
management readers are checked. Preflight is an internal implementation step.
Construction uses the running manager's retained Python and static BusyBox;
candidate code is not executed. See docs/system-transition.md.
.PP
.B system status
reports installed generations, retained incomplete generations, components and
explicit running/next-boot identities separately. A matching human label does
not identify a native artifact. --verify audits retained archives and profile
content; --json returns mpm-system-status-v1. Plain status creates no registry
or locks and needs no codec. Installation does not imply boot readiness.
.PP
System actions use --disk-root DIRECTORY, with the same installed/offline target
rules as Base. Global --root is rejected. Installation requires an administrator,
preserves current configuration, Base/Kernel, boot choices and running services,
and does not publish an upgrade wish. See docs/system-installation.md for scope.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --root " DIRECTORY"
Dedicated registry/profile directory; Ports defaults to /var/lib/mojt/ports.
The source frontend's standalone Kernel default is /var/lib/mojt/kernel;
the installed frontend defaults to the mounted disk's Kernel registry.
Use a new empty directory for experiments. Never move an initialized root:
Nix indirect garbage-collection roots refer to its original location.
.SH SOURCE AND CACHE ACTIONS
.TP
.BI "source add " "NAME --from-file SOURCE --policy-file POLICY [--replace]"
Register an exact local native source and adopt its protected owner policy.
A new source needs an explicit metadata update. Repeating the same registration
preserves its snapshot; changing source/policy needs --replace and resets current
metadata. Previous records and snapshots remain retained. Editing the original
policy file does not change the adopted policy; reconfigure explicitly.
.TP
.BI "source update " "NAME"
Accept snapshot.json, catalog.json and acquisition-map.json under the registered
local repository root, together with verified external descriptors. Optional
--snapshot-file, --catalog-file and --acquisition-file select confined relative
metadata paths. Publish all accepted bytes before one current snapshot pointer.
An invalid refresh preserves the last good snapshot. No payload is fetched,
installed or activated. Initially supports one native release catalog per snapshot.
.TP
.BI "source list " "[--json]"
List registered names and accepted snapshot IDs without refresh or providers.
.TP
.BI "source show " "NAME [--snapshot ID] [--json]"
Show the exact accepted registration, snapshot and retained catalog. Report pending
refresh and local directory presence; snapshot age is unknown. An old snapshot
must still match the currently registered source and policy.
.TP
.BI "cache fetch " "ARTIFACT --source NAME --kind KIND --archive-sha256 HEX"
Acquire one full native sha256 artifact ID with an approved compressed encoding.
KIND is base, system, kernel or port. --snapshot ID pins a retained snapshot;
otherwise use current accepted metadata. Verify the complete archive before cache
publication and retain acquisition evidence. This does not install or deploy it.
.PP
Source/cache mutation supports --dry-run and -y. Preview writes nothing and does
not verify the archive body. Normal mutation asks for confirmation unless -y.
Installed mutation requires an administrator. Source registry defaults to
/var/lib/mojt/sources; cache defaults to /var/lib/mojt/cache. --root selects the
registry for source commands and cache for cache commands; --sources-root on
cache fetch selects its source registry. Parents must already exist.
.PP
Source trust comes from explicit protected local owner policy, not SHA256 alone.
Remote native sources and signing are not provided. Exact Kernel installation
can consume a named source through the verified cache, as described below;
Base/System/native Port installation joins remain work. Existing explicit Ports
source bundles continue to work separately.
.SH PORTS ACTIONS
.TP
.B update
On an installed System, check its pinned provider source. On a fresh machine,
accept an empty named nixpkgs view; packages are discovered on demand by install.
An existing view keeps its accepted pin. This does not fetch a newer Nixpkgs
revision or advance installed packages. An explicit bundle is needed to change
an accepted pin in this implementation.
.TP
.BI "update --sources " "FILE [--expected-sources ID]"
Accept an explicit local mojt-ports-sources-v1 bundle for this machine's platform.
The envelope has format, id and description; description has platform and sources.
The content ID binds all catalogs and exact adapter offers. --expected-sources
requires that exact ID. This is operator acceptance, not signature authentication.
Update changes saved metadata only: accepted request targets and active commands
stay unchanged, even if the new catalog withdraws an installed release.
There is no channel refresh, package evaluation or implicit source build.
.TP
.BI "install " "SELECTOR ... [--source NAME] [--no-activate]"
Use the common resolver for stable latest NAME, numeric series NAME@2.12 or
exact release NAME@=2.12.3. On an installed System, no initial --sources file is
needed: missing package names are evaluated against /run/mojt-system/ports.toml.
Only that pinned source's offered releases are available; an unavailable exact
release or series fails before payload acquisition. The adapter supplies a stable
release policy for this source; opaque versions remain exact-only.
The source-checkout frontend needs --sources FILE, or an already accepted view.
Multiple named sources require --source NAME.
Record independent interactive wishes and import exact binary objects.
Existing accepted wishes do not advance as a side effect of another install.
First unambiguous installation records a following default unless --no-activate;
later installations retain versions without changing an existing active choice.
.PP
Provider discovery uses no legacy Ports state. It temporarily retains exact
signed binary outputs to inventory their actual commands, then passes a finite
catalog and exact references to the same profile transaction as explicit bundles.
No source builds are allowed. Without -y, downloading and final publication have
separate confirmations. Failure leaves the previous profile current; temporary
roots are released, though downloaded Nix objects can remain for later reuse.
For a newly discovered name, --dry-run reports an acquisition preview: commands
and aliases await binary inspection. It creates no profile or roots and imports
no payloads. It is not a publishable profile plan.
.TP
.BI "upgrade " "[NAME ...]"
Advance only recorded latest/series wishes for those names, or all wishes if
no names are supplied. Installed artifacts alone are not upgrade wishes.
Exact-release wishes keep their accepted full build, including after withdrawal.
An exact active override stays pinned while a following wish may advance.
Older owned versions, old profiles and provider roots remain retained.
Use an explicit update or --sources FILE to accept another catalog snapshot.
.TP
.BI "activate " "INSTALLED_SELECTOR_OR_REFERENCE [--follow]"
Resolve using installed release evidence, never the current catalog or downloads.
An exact release or full sha256 reference ID from list --exact pins the default.
A latest/series selector follows one matching accepted interactive wish. --follow
also permits an exact reference if exactly one following wish currently targets
it. Install the matching interactive wish first if the target is only owned by
configuration. Ambiguous installed builds require the full reference ID.
.TP
.BI "apply --configuration " "FILE"
Apply only Ports wishes from a versioned mojt-configuration-v1 TOML document.
Base, System and Kernel sections are rejected in this bounded implementation.
A named configuration releases only its own removed wishes/defaults. Independent
interactive wishes and overrides survive. Configuration reapplication resolves
its own eligible wishes; unrelated interactive wishes keep accepted targets.
Logical removal does not physically collect files or older profiles.
.TP
.BI "remove " "SELECTOR ... [--source NAME]"
Release interactive wishes only. A bare NAME releases all interactive wishes and
active overrides for that role, then restores any explicit adopted configuration
default. A version/series-qualified selector releases only that wish and preserves
an independent exact active pin. --source restricts released wishes to that binding
and preserves independent exact overrides. Configuration-owned wishes stay owned;
edit and apply their configuration to release those reasons.
This is logical reason removal, not physical artifact collection. Historical
profiles, provider roots, shared dependencies and user data remain retained.
A repeated release of absent interactive reasons is a no-op. Single-artifact
pruning by full reference ID is not implemented by this command.
.TP
.BI "reset " "NAME ..."
Clear only interactive active overrides for these bare roles. Keep all install
wishes and restore explicit defaults from already adopted configuration documents.
No file is reread, source refreshed or accepted target upgraded. If configuration
choices conflict when the override is released, refuse the whole operation.
Without an explicit configured default, ordinary retained-version fallback applies.
Repeated reset with no override is a no-op. This is not a whole-system reset or
an instruction to forget a configuration source.
.TP
.BR "list " [ --exact | --json ]
Show logically installed versions and active defaults. --exact includes the full
provider-independent sha256 reference ID usable for activation. --json includes
owning claims, declared release evidence, exports and install/activation ordering.
.TP
.B status
Show the current profile identity, installed versions, normalized wishes,
effective defaults, aliases and source snapshots as JSON. Does not import a
provider or rehash payload bytes. No profile is created for an empty target.
.TP
.BI "history " "[--json] [--limit N]"
Show committed linked transactions, newest first; default 20, range 1 to 1000.
Records bind before/after profile and request identities, package changes, aliases,
sequence, UTC and actor UID. This is neither an undo command nor a tamper-proof log.
.TP
.BI "search " "FRAGMENT [--limit N] [--json]"
Literal case-insensitive name substring search in accepted local source catalogs.
No regular expression and no network request. Default limit 50, maximum 200.
.TP
.BI "info " "INSTALLED_SELECTOR_OR_REFERENCE"
Show installed release evidence and exact reference without provider execution.
.TP
.BI "resolve " "SELECTOR --catalog FILE [--json]"
Read-only resolution from an explicit mpm-release-catalog-v1 file, without a
provider, installation, metadata adoption or source refresh.
.PP
Exports include active command names, requested COMMAND-SERIES aliases and exact
COMMAND-RELEASE-FULL_REFERENCE_HASH aliases for logically retained versions.
Conflicting exports, unsupported names and management-command shadowing fail
before publication. Installed unknown/preview stability remains exact-only;
no digest sorting or inferred version order breaks a build tie.
.PP
Fresh Ports dispatch does not convert experimental state, use attribute fallback,
or expose the old metadata-adoption interface. Artifact pruning and explicit
configuration-source forgetting remain implementation work. Native Port archives and remote
source acquisition are not connected yet. The optional Nix catalog exporter's
--bundle mode prepares this input from already-local outputs without importing
or retaining anything; it is not a remote catalog downloader.
.SH MUTATION OPTIONS
All mutations accept --json and the following options. update, install, upgrade,
activate and apply additionally accept --sources FILE / --expected-sources ID;
remove and reset operate solely on already accepted state.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Preview without commands, payload import, roots or state writes. Exact exports
are declared in adapter identities and physically verified after acquisition.
.TP
.BR -y , " --yes"
Apply the displayed plan without an interactive confirmation. Does not expand
scope to Base, System, Kernel or another source snapshot.
.SH RECOVERY AND COMPATIBILITY
Mutations are serialized with a lock. A complete profile, state.json and
transaction.json are flushed before atomically switching current. Failures
before the switch preserve the old profile; downloaded objects may remain.
A failure after the switch is reported as possibly committed and the profile
is retained. Complete backend power-loss recovery is not established.
.PP
Only fresh versioned stores are accepted. No legacy conversion is performed.
System capability metadata declares mojt-ports-profile-v1 and
mpm-request-origins-v1; a candidate without the required reader/writer support
must be refused. Shared Ports state is not rolled back with System selection.
.PP
After changing command availability, the current shell may need
.BR "hash -r" .
There is no general undo, physical GC, full power-loss recovery or implicit
reconciliation of direct Nix changes. Content verification still runs on an
unchanged apply; an unchanged plan creates no new profile or history event.
.SH RELATION TO SYSTEM UPGRADE
mpm system upgrade --artifact ID selects an exact installed immutable generation
through the shared native-v2 update path. Its baseline is the selected deployment,
which may differ from the running one. Moving-policy resolution is not implemented.
Ports upgrade changes the current shared application profile immediately.
Neither operation may silently change the other area's requests, catalog or
activation, nor Base or Kernel selections. Sharing immutable Nix objects
does not merge those policies. The updater checks whether
its bundled MPM can read/write the selected persistent metadata format;
mutable account, home and application state is not automatically rolled back.
.SH LOCAL DISCOVERY PROTOTYPE
The repository frontend supports catalog search, show and versions with an
explicit local --catalog discovery envelope. Area-scoped forms include
ports search, ports show and ports versions with that option. Ordinary Ports
search without a discovery catalog retains its existing Nix source semantics.
The installed System launcher delivers the same frontend and discovery reader.
.PP
Discovery search matches literal names and declared aliases, case-insensitively.
Use --exact for a whole name/alias, --description to additionally search
descriptions, and --json for structured search output. There is no implicit
regexp, network access or installed-state adoption. One row groups a package's
builds; source evidence remains visible.
.PP
The read-only kernel resolve command requires --catalog, --base-context and
--policy pinned, latest-lts or latest-upstream. Pinned takes --series major.minor
or --release exact-release. All policies apply declared selected-Base limits;
an impossible request fails without a policy or Base fallback. This does not
install a kernel, persist its policy or change boot selection. See
docs/mpm-catalog.md for synthetic examples and the experimental schema.
.SH EXACT KERNEL INSTALL AND STATUS
The repository frontend supports kernel install --from-file ARCHIVE
--expected-id sha256:FULL_HEX_ID, with optional --dry-run or -y, and kernel
status with optional --verify and --json. Use --root DIRECTORY before kernel
or after the action, once only. Default registry: /var/lib/mojt/kernel;
shared mutations require an administrator. A private user-owned root is supported.
.PP
Installation retains a verified archive, private read-only Kernel tree and
installation record by exact artifact ID. Repeating it checks retained content
and makes no change. Unsupported dependencies and requirements are rejected.
Status distinguishes installed content from unknown selected deployment and
the actual running release; uname alone does not establish artifact identity.
No boot selection or running module tree changes. Deployment compatibility is
unevaluated. See docs/mpm-kernel.md for the supported local subset and limits.
.PP
Alternatively use kernel install --source NAME --expected-id sha256:FULL_ID
--archive-sha256 HEX, with optional --snapshot ID, --sources-root and --cache-root.
This joins an accepted native source snapshot and verified archive cache to the
same Kernel installer. Source and file input modes are mutually exclusive.
Eligibility is checked before acquisition; source-mode --dry-run writes nothing
and does not read the archive body. Actual import re-verifies a private frozen
copy and retains the source ID, snapshot/cache verification record and exact
encoding as origin evidence. Installation remains separate from deployment.
.PP
An already installed artifact is scrubbed and remains a no-op, retaining its
original origin record without needing source archive/cache bytes. Source or
policy reconfiguration invalidates the old authorization. A metadata-only refresh
does not replace an explicitly planned snapshot. On failure, verified cached
bytes may remain even though no Kernel object was installed. See
docs/kernel-source-install.md and docs/kernel-source-boot.md for the recorded
owner installation and exact-deployment reboot acceptance.
.SH READ-ONLY KERNEL DEPLOYMENT PLAN
kernel plan --request FILE [--json] checks a complete explicit local plan.
Use --root DIRECTORY before kernel or after plan, once only, to name the Kernel
registry. No registry, lock, selection, cache or boot files are created.
.PP
The exact candidate must be installed and pass a fresh integrity scrub.
The request binds selected Base and System identities, Base Kernel limits,
System ABI/state/Ports/shell facts and an initramfs checksum/binding to the exact
Kernel image/module inventory, filesystem UUID and console. Base/System userspace
must remain selected unchanged; missing or incompatible evidence fails.
No running release, current link, provider or alternate Base is used as fallback.
.PP
Successful plans still report boot_ready false. Explicit metadata is not an
authenticated disk selection; Base/System payloads, actual initramfs contents,
hardware/firmware, Secure Boot and runtime ABI remain unaudited. The planner
does not generate initramfs, write GRUB or modify selected/running state.
See docs/kernel-deployment-plan.md for strict experimental schemas and limits.
.SH EXPERIMENTAL NATIVE-DISK KERNEL BOOT
The checkout frontend supports kernel stage, select, rollback and status with
--disk-root DIRECTORY. Do not combine this with the standalone registry --root.
This first subset supports an accepted native serial UEFI/VirtIO disk, not an
arbitrary installation. Use --baseline boot/mojt/SYSTEM/BASE for its original
explicit boot pair; customized or mismatched menus are rejected.
.PP
stage requires --artifact sha256:FULL_KERNEL_ID naming an installed exact Kernel.
It generates a new immutable boot slot without changing next boot or running
content. select requires --deployment sha256:FULL_DEPLOYMENT_ID and
--expected-selected sha256:CURRENT_DEPLOYMENT_ID. rollback requires the same
expected-selection guard and selects the previously selected entry. Mutations
require an administrator; use --dry-run for preview and -y to confirm.
.PP
status --disk-root reports selected, previous, staged and installed identities.
Add --running-record /run/mojt/deployment.json in the guest for explicit runtime
identity; uname alone is insufficient. Selection changes next boot only. The old
deployment remains available; no reboot, collection or provider acquisition is
implicit. See docs/kernel-boot-selection.md for verification and recovery limits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/current
Atomic selection of an internal retained profile.
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/profiles/*/state.json
Authoritative snapshot of Ports metadata for that profile.
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/profiles/*/transaction.json
Transaction and ancestry, published with its snapshot. No separate database.
.TP
.B /var/lib/mojt/ports/retained/nix
Stable provider retention roots, outside disposable profile staging.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mpm ports update --sources hello-sources.json --dry-run
mpm ports update --sources hello-sources.json -y
mpm ports install hello@2.12 -y
mpm ports upgrade hello --dry-run
mpm ports upgrade hello -y
mpm ports list --exact
mpm ports activate 'sha256:REFERENCE_FROM_LIST' -y
mpm ports activate hello@2.12 -y
mpm ports apply --configuration mojt-configuration.toml --dry-run
mpm ports remove hello@2.12 --dry-run
mpm ports reset hello --dry-run
mpm ports history
man mpm
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, cancellation or an unchanged plan; nonzero on failure.
Shared installation changes require an administrator in the installed System.
Ordinary users may inspect catalogs, Ports status/history/search and Kernel
status/plans. Area-specific ownership and compatibility checks still apply.
.SH SEE ALSO
